[175.166147, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\n# Baseline and Configuration Weakness Report\n\n## 1. Overview\nA security baseline evaluation was conducted by comparing findings in `/sas/baseline.json:1` and system configurations in `/disk` against Debian 12 Linux hardening benchmarks and Redis security guidance.\n\n## 2. Identified Configuration Weaknesses\n\n### Baseline Weakness 1: Insecure Redis Service Exposure\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3` (`Redis protected-mode no; bind 0.0.0.0; no password set`)\n- **Check Reference**: `CHK-05` (`/sas/baseline.json:2`)\n- **Defect Description**:\n  - `bind 0.0.0.0` allows connections on all network interfaces without firewall or interface restrictions.\n  - `protected-mode no` overrides Redis' built-in protection mechanism that rejects non-loopback queries when no password is set.\n  - `requirepass` is completely absent, granting unauthenticated administrative access to anyone reachable on port 6379.\n- **Risk Impact**: High. Direct execution of administrative commands (`CONFIG`, `MODULE LOAD`, `SAVE`) leading to root system compromise.\n\n### Baseline Weakness 2: Service Privilege Execution Context\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5` and `/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1`\n- **Defect Description**: The Redis daemon was running under `root` privileges (or had elevated access allowing write access to system directories like `/var/spool/cron/crontabs/` and `/etc/ld.so.preload`).\n- **Risk Impact**: Critical. Allows arbitrary file writes directly to system configuration and cron directories.\n\n### Baseline Weakness 3: Dynamic Linker Preload Security Gap\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/ld.so.preload:1`\n- **Defect Description**: System file permissions and integrity monitoring failed to prevent or detect modifications to `/etc/ld.so.preload` pointing to unauthorized libraries (`/lib/.hide.so`).\n- **Risk Impact**: High. Facilitates rootkit persistence and evasive process hiding.\n\n### Baseline Weakness 4: Absence of Network Boundary & Ingress Controls\n- **Evidence**: `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`\n- **Defect Description**: Inbound network connections from untrusted public IP ranges (`198.51.100.170`) to management/database ports (6379) were not filtered at host firewall (`nftables`/`iptables`) or perimeter firewall levels.\n- **Risk Impact**: High. Allows direct external exposure of sensitive internal microservices.\n\n## 3. Remediation Baseline Requirements\n1. Enforce strict `bind 127.0.0.1` or internal private IP binding in `/etc/redis/redis.conf`.\n2. Re-enable `protected-mode yes`.\n3. Configure strong authentication with `requirepass` or ACL user roles.\n4. Rename or disable dangerous Redis commands (`CONFIG`, `MODULE`, `FLUSHALL`, `KEYS`).\n5. Run Redis under a dedicated unprivileged user (`redis:redis`) with minimal filesystem permissions.\n6. Deploy File Integrity Monitoring (FIM) on `/etc/ld.so.preload`, `/etc/crontab`, and cron directories.\nEOF\n"]
[175.166531, "o", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
